ever since Edwin Hubble discovered that  the universe is expanding there has been  a great debate about the speed of the  suspension also called the Hubble  constant in 2000 scientists thought they  had reached consensus with the Hubble  constant at about 72 but since then two  methods of calculating the expansion  rate have gotten farther and farther  apart and some scientists think it's too  big of a difference to just be a  statistical clue to calculate the Hubble  constant we need both a velocity and a  difference velocities are easy it  requires measuring the redshift of a  galaxy as it receives but distances are  much harder we need standard candles  objects that shine with an intrinsic  brightness that way it's observed  brightness could be converted to a  distance the first method used to  calculate the Hubble constant originated  with Hubble himself he started by  measuring distances to nearby objects  and working his way out hence the name  the cosmic distance letter the first  rung is within our Milky Way here we  measure the distances to seppius  pulsating stars that have an intrinsic  brightness then we look to nearby  galaxies that contain both sethius and  another type of standard candle type 1a  supernova these exploding white dwarf  stars also have an intrinsic brightness  after calibrating these supernovae  astronomers can then move out to a third  rung - even more distant galaxies that  only contains supernovae linking all  these measurements a recent study has  pinned the Hubble constant at 73  instead of starting within our galaxy  and looking out the second method looks  back in time to almost the beginning of  the universe here we find the Cosmic  Microwave Background the radiation left  over from the Big Bang the CMB subtle  variations in temperature described  changes in the density of the universe  right after the Big Bang instead of  standard candles physicists use the size  of the tiny fluctuations as standard  yard surface with these yardstick  they can calculate what the hubble  constant would be today recent estimates  are at about 68 significantly lower than  the cosmic distance ladder approach both  camps hope to resolve this debate in the  coming years with the help of new  telescopes and instruments but if the  discrepancy in the Hubble constant  remains it could mean that our  understanding of the expansion of the  universe is missing and agrees that  something happens between the Big Bang  and President  maybe there's a neutrino out there we  haven't discovered maybe the mysterious  force dark energy which makes up a  significant portion of our universe can  change in strength or maybe subtle flaws  in one or both approaches to the Hubble  constant are giving us the wrong answer  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

